WEBVTT

00:05.800 --> 00:13.090
It may not be immediately obvious, but exponents like these are really just an extension of what we

00:13.090 --> 00:17.320
already know about basic arithmetic rules, like addition and multiplication.

00:17.830 --> 00:22.660
Exponents can be a little confusing at first, but they're actually a huge convenience for us, and

00:22.660 --> 00:27.370
that's because they're basically just a shortcut for expressing repeated multiplication.

00:27.910 --> 00:33.910
For instance, if we want to multiply two by itself ten times without exponents, we have to write this

00:33.910 --> 00:39.890
as two times, two times, two times, two times two, etc., where we multiply it by two, ten times.

00:39.910 --> 00:47.080
But with exponents we can take this big, long, messy multiplication and just simply write it as two

00:47.080 --> 00:50.620
to the 10th or two to the power of ten or two to the ten power.

00:51.400 --> 00:53.580
This is why we want to learn about exponents.

00:53.590 --> 00:56.950
They make some math expressions much, much easier.

00:56.950 --> 01:00.160
So we'll start this lesson group by introducing exponents.

01:00.160 --> 01:04.480
Then we'll talk about rules of exponents so that you know how to work with them.
